+
+# Conform applyPatch to RFC 6902
+
+## Request
+
+Users of `applyPatch` report surprising results. Some patches they consider invalid are accepted, some
+edge cases give results they did not expect, and a patch that fails does not always behave the way they
+read the standard. Review how patches are checked and applied, and make the behaviour conform to
+RFC 6902 in every case, errors included.
+
+## Acceptance
+
+- A patch that is not valid JSON Patch throws a `SyntaxError` before any operation runs, whatever the
+  position of the faulty operation: this includes a malformed `path` or `from` pointer, and a `value`
+  that is not a JSON value (such as `undefined`, a function, `NaN`, or a `Date`).
+- A sparse patch array, or an operation that is inherited rather than an own member, is handled as an
+  invalid patch and throws a `SyntaxError`, not a `TypeError` or a silent acceptance.
+- `move` of a location onto itself is a no-op when the location exists, including the whole document.
+- Existing tests in `test/apply-patch.test.ts` and `test/resolve-pointer.test.ts` keep passing; new
+  tests cover each point above.
+- README's JSON Patch section matches the behaviour.
+
+## Outcome
+
+Reviewed `applyPatch` against RFC 6902 and reproduced the faults before changing anything:
+
+- A sparse patch array threw a `TypeError` (the `forEach` skipped the hole, then `op` was read from `undefined`).
+- A `value` of `undefined`, `NaN` or a `Date` was accepted; `undefined` and `Date` silently became `{}`,
+  `NaN` became `null`.
+- A malformed pointer in a later operation was reported only when reached, after an earlier operation had
+  already failed with an `Error`, so the error class depended on the operation order.
+- `move` of the whole document onto itself threw, though RFC 6902 §4.4 makes it a no-op.
+- Operation members were read through the prototype chain, so an inherited `op` or `path` was accepted.
+
+Changes in `src/index.ts`:
+
+- `checkOperation` now reads only own members, parses the `path` and `from` pointers, and requires a
+  JSON value (`isJsonValue`: no `undefined`, non-finite number, sparse array or class instance). Every
+  operation is checked before any runs, so these are `SyntaxError`s whatever the position.
+- The check loop uses `for...of`, so holes are visited and rejected.
+- `move` onto the same location returns the document after checking that the location exists.
+
+Tests: `test/apply-patch.test.ts` gains 6 tests (74 in total, all passing with `npm test`). The source
+type-checks under `tsc --strict` (checked with a temporary TypeScript install outside the repository).
+README's JSON Patch section describes the checks and the `move` case.
+
+Not changed: the input `document` is still copied without validation, so a non-JSON value inside it (for
+example a `Date`) is still turned into `{}`. A cyclic `value` is still not detected. Both are outside the
+patch checks above and are left for a separate decision.
